The node equations solved cleanly. Now the dependent quantities — the Thevenin voltage itself, the balance checks, and the short-circuit cross-checks:
{"type":"hint","text":"Make the bottom rail your reference node — it is terminal c — and one nodal analysis with a-b open hands you the open-circuit voltage for both parts at once. For the resistances there are no dependent sources, so Section 4.5's first case applies: turn the 24 V and 2 A sources off and combine resistors [p. 138]."}
{"type":"statement","kind":"theorem","number":"4.5","name":"Thevenin's theorem","page":137,"text":"A linear two-terminal circuit can be replaced by an equivalent circuit consisting of a voltage source \\(V_{Th}\\) in series with a resistor \\(R_{Th}\\), where \\(V_{Th}\\) is the open-circuit voltage at the terminals and \\(R_{Th}\\) is the input (equivalent) resistance at the terminals when the independent sources are turned off."}
{"type":"part","label":"(a)","title":"Thevenin equivalent seen from a-b"}
{"type":"step","title":"Label the nodes, with c as the reference"}
{"type":"para","text":"Let the bottom rail be ground: it is terminal c, and the 2 Ω under the 24 V source, the 5 Ω and the 2 A source all return to it. Call \\(V_1\\) the junction of the 3 Ω, 1 Ω and 4 Ω, and \\(V_2\\) the node at terminal b. With a-b open, no current can enter or leave at a, so **the 1 Ω carries no current** and terminal a sits at \\(V_1\\) — that will matter when we read off \\(V_{Th}\\) (Eq. 4.6) [p. 137]."}
{"type":"para","text":"The left branch is the 24 V source in series with the 2 Ω, so the same current runs through both: seen from node 1 it is just \\(3+2 = 5\\ \\Omega\\) leading to a 24 V source whose + faces node 1."}
{"type":"step","title":"Write the two open-circuit node equations"}
{"type":"para","text":"Sum the currents leaving each node; the injected 2 A moves to the right-hand side of node b's equation:"}
{"type":"math","tex":"\\begin{aligned}\\frac{V_1 - 24}{3+2} + \\frac{V_1 - V_2}{4} &= 0 && \\text{(node 1)}\\\\[2pt]\\frac{V_2 - V_1}{4} + \\frac{V_2}{5} &= 2 && \\text{(node b)}\\end{aligned}"}
{"type":"step","title":"Solve for the node voltages"}
{"type":"para","text":"Two equations, two unknowns — elimination or a calculator both work:"}
{"type":"math","tex":"V_1 = 19\\ \\text{V}, \\qquad V_2 = 15\\ \\text{V}"}
{"type":"note","text":"Plug the pair back in to be sure it balances: node 1 sums to \\((19-24)/(3+2) + (19-15)/4 = 0\\), and node b's resistors carry \\((15-19)/4 + 15/5 = 2\\ \\text{A}\\), exactly what the source injects."}
{"type":"step","title":"Read off V_Th from a to b"}
{"type":"para","text":"\\(V_{Th}\\) is the open-circuit voltage between the terminals (Eq. 4.6) [p. 137]. Since the dormant 1 Ω has no drop, a rides on node 1 while b is node 2:"}
{"type":"derivation","steps":[{"tex":"V_{Th} = v_{ab} = V_1 - V_2","why":"Terminal a sits at node 1 (nothing flows in the 1 Ω) and b is node 2, measured from the c rail."},{"tex":"= 19 - 15 = 4\\ \\text{V}","why":"Node voltages from the solve; a comes out the positive terminal."}]}
{"type":"step","title":"Kill the sources and look into a-b"}
{"type":"para","text":"No dependent sources are present, so the book's CASE 1 applies: the 24 V source becomes a wire and the 2 A source an open circuit [p. 138]. The dead left branch leaves \\(3+2 = 5\\ \\Omega\\) from node 1 to the rail (a source in series keeps its resistor), and the 5 Ω that paralleled the 2 A source stays. From node 1 you can reach b two ways: straight through the 4 Ω, or down \\(3+2\\) to the rail and back up the 5 Ω — \\(10\\ \\Omega\\) all told."}
{"type":"derivation","steps":[{"tex":"R_{1b} = 4 \\,\\|\\, \\bigl((3+2)+5\\bigr) = 4 \\,\\|\\, 10 = \\tfrac{20}{7}\\ \\Omega","why":"Two parallel paths from node 1 to node b: the 4 Ω, or the rail route through the dead left branch and the 5 Ω."},{"tex":"R_{Th} = 1 + \\tfrac{20}{7} = \\tfrac{27}{7}\\ \\Omega","why":"Terminal a reaches node 1 only through the 1 Ω, which is in series with everything else."}]}
{"type":"callout","tone":"check","title":"Cross-check with i_sc","text":"Section 4.5 also notes \\(R_{Th} = v_{oc}/i_{sc}\\) [p. 138]. Short a-b and solve the same two nodes again: the short carries \\(i_{sc} = \\tfrac{28}{27}\\ \\text{A}\\) from a to b, and \\(V_{Th}/i_{sc} = 4/\\tfrac{28}{27} = \\tfrac{27}{7}\\ \\Omega\\) — the same resistance by a completely different route."}
{"type":"answer","label":"(a)","text":"\\(V_{Th} = 4\\ \\text{V}\\) with a positive with respect to b, and \\(R_{Th} = \\dfrac{27}{7}\\ \\Omega \\approx 3.86\\ \\Omega\\)."}
{"type":"part","label":"(b)","title":"Thevenin equivalent seen from b-c"}
{"type":"step","title":"The open-circuit voltage is already in hand"}
{"type":"para","text":"Renaming the terminals changes nothing inside the circuit: with a-b open the node voltages are exactly the ones solved in part (a), and the reference rail is terminal c itself. So b's open-circuit potential is simply \\(V_2\\):"}
{"type":"derivation","steps":[{"tex":"V_{Th} = v_{bc} = V_2 - 0","why":"Terminal c is the reference node and b is node 2."},{"tex":"= 15\\ \\text{V}","why":"From the same nodal solve as part (a); b is the positive terminal."}]}
{"type":"note","text":"This is the payoff of choosing c as the reference from the start: one analysis serves both parts. It also explains why the values differ — with the 1 Ω dormant, \\(v_{ab}\\) is only the drop across the 4 Ω (1 A through it, 4 V), while \\(v_{bc}\\) is node b's whole potential above the rail."}
{"type":"step","title":"Kill the sources and look into b-c"}
{"type":"para","text":"Same dead network as before: the shorted 24 V leaves \\(3+2 = 5\\ \\Omega\\) from node 1 to the rail, and the opened 2 A leaves the 5 Ω in place. From b, terminal c is reached through the 5 Ω directly, or through the 4 Ω to node 1 and then the \\(3+2\\) branch down to the rail — \\(9\\ \\Omega\\) all told. The 1 Ω now dangles at open terminal a, so it carries nothing and drops out:"}
{"type":"derivation","steps":[{"tex":"R_{Th} = 5 \\,\\|\\, \\bigl(4+(3+2)\\bigr) = 5 \\,\\|\\, 9","why":"Two parallel paths from b to the rail; the 1 Ω is out because terminal a is open."},{"tex":"= \\tfrac{45}{14}\\ \\Omega","why":"Product over sum for the parallel pair."}]}
{"type":"callout","tone":"caveat","title":"The 1 Ω is a decoy in this part","text":"Looking in from b-c, do not fold the 1 Ω into the resistance: its far end is terminal a, which is left open, so no current can ever flow through it. It matters only from a-b, where it is the first resistor you meet."}
{"type":"note","text":"Cross-check the same way as part (a): shorting b-c forces \\(V_2 = 0\\), node 1 then sits at \\(V_1 = 32/3\\ \\text{V}\\), the short carries \\(i_{sc} = (32/3)/4 + 2 = \\tfrac{14}{3}\\ \\text{A}\\), and \\(v_{oc}/i_{sc} = 15/\\tfrac{14}{3} = \\tfrac{45}{14}\\ \\Omega\\)."}
{"type":"answer","label":"(b)","text":"\\(V_{Th} = 15\\ \\text{V}\\) with b positive with respect to c, and \\(R_{Th} = \\dfrac{45}{14}\\ \\Omega \\approx 3.21\\ \\Omega\\)."}